Microsoft Learn: Configure Self-Service Password Reset (SSPR) in Microsoft Entra ID

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Self-Service Password Reset in Microsoft Entra ID
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to configure and manage Self-Service Password Reset (SSPR) in Microsoft Entra ID to reduce administrative workload and improve user productivity by allowing users to reset their own passwords securely.

This module focused on evaluating SSPR requirements, configuring password reset policies, and customizing the password reset experience for users in an enterprise environment.

Step 1: Understanding Self-Service Password Reset (SSPR)

The module introduced Self-Service Password Reset (SSPR) as a Microsoft Entra ID feature that allows users to reset or unlock their passwords without requiring assistance from IT administrators.

Common challenges addressed by SSPR:
High volume of password reset requests
Increased help desk workload
Reduced employee productivity
Delays in regaining account access
Benefits of SSPR:
Reduced support costs
Faster password recovery
Improved user productivity
Increased operational efficiency
Enhanced account security

The learning scenario focused on a large retail organization struggling with manual password reset requests from employees.

Step 2: Evaluating SSPR Requirements

The training covered the requirements needed before implementing Self-Service Password Reset.

Required components:
Requirement	Description
Microsoft Entra ID P1 License	Required for SSPR functionality
Microsoft Entra Tenant	Identity management environment
Authentication Policy Administrator Role	Required for configuration tasks
Important lessons learned:
SSPR is not available with free Microsoft Entra subscriptions
Organizations require eligible licensing plans
Proper administrative permissions are required for deployment

The module emphasized evaluating licensing and administrative prerequisites before implementation.

Step 3: Enabling Self-Service Password Reset

The training demonstrated how administrators enable SSPR within Microsoft Entra ID.

Configuration options:
Option	Purpose
None	SSPR disabled
Selected	Enable SSPR for specific users or groups
All	Enable SSPR for all users
Key concepts learned:
Organizations can perform phased deployments
Pilot groups can be used for testing
SSPR can be gradually expanded to all users

The module highlighted the importance of controlled deployment and validation.

Step 4: Configuring Authentication Methods

A major focus of the module was configuring authentication methods used to verify user identity during password reset.

Available verification methods:
Mobile phone
Email
Security questions
Microsoft Authenticator
Office phone
Benefits:
Secure identity verification
Reduced risk of unauthorized password resets
Flexible user authentication options
Key lessons learned:
Multiple authentication methods improve security
Organizations can define how many methods are required
Verification settings should align with security policies

Authentication methods play a critical role in securing the password reset process.

Step 5: User Registration Requirements

The module introduced registration requirements for SSPR.

Before users can reset their passwords, they must register their authentication information.

Registration information may include:
Mobile phone number
Alternate email address
Microsoft Authenticator configuration
Benefits:
Supports secure identity verification
Improves password recovery success rates
Strengthens account security

The module emphasized encouraging users to complete registration before requiring password reset functionality.

